I added request logging in a new file, `proxy.ts`, in the project root. In this version of Next.js (16.4 canary), the old `middleware` file is deprecated and has been renamed to `proxy`, so I used the new name. It runs on the Node.js runtime by default.

```ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'

// No `matcher` is exported, so this runs on every request
// (pages, API routes, static assets, and images).
export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  console.log(
    `[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${request.method} ${request.nextUrl.pathname}${request.nextUrl.search}`
  )
  return NextResponse.next()
}
```

I tested it with `next dev` and it logged both requests I sent:
```
[2026-09-22T20:42:03.451Z] GET /?x=1
[2026-09-22T20:42:05.200Z] GET /nope
```

- **What gets logged:** there's no `matcher`, so every request is logged, including `_next/static`, `_next/image` and files in `public/`. If you only want page and API requests, add a matcher to cut the noise:
  ```ts
  export const config = {
    matcher: ['/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)'],
  }
  ```
- **Files the dev server changed:** running `next dev` rewrote `tsconfig.json` and created `.next/` and `next-env.d.ts`. I reverted all three. It also replaced the text in your untracked `AGENTS.md` with Next.js's own generated version, which has the same guidance in slightly different words. I left that as is, because `next dev` writes it again every time it runs.

